course: biology
lesson: 366
title: Odd-looking animals: shoebills, star-nosed moles, saigas, pangolins and hagfish
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some animals look as if a child drew them for a joke: a bird with a shoe for a beak, a mole with a pink star on its nose, an antelope with a little trunk, a walking pine cone and a fish that turns into slime.
explain: But every odd part is a tool: the star feels for worms in the dark, the big nose warms freezing air, the scales are armour and the slime chokes a shark's gills.
explain: Think of a toolbox: a strange-looking tool is strange because it does one job very well, and these animals are the same.
explain: Looks can also fool us: the shoebill looks like a stork and a pangolin looks like a reptile, but scientists check relatives by genes and bodies, not by looks.
fact: The shoebill lives in swamps of eastern and central Africa, stands about as tall as a ten-year-old and waits almost without moving before it lunges to grab fish such as lungfish.
fact: Shoebills greet each other by clattering their huge beaks, and scientists now think their closest relatives are pelicans and hammerkops, not true storks.
fact: Only a few thousand shoebills are left in the wild because swamps are drained and eggs are taken, and despite their scary stare they are shy birds.
fact: The star-nosed mole's star has 22 fleshy rays, 11 on each side, with about 25,000 tiny touch sensors, and it can find and eat a tiny meal in about a quarter of a second.
fact: The star-nosed mole swims in North American wetlands and smells underwater by blowing little bubbles onto things and sniffing them back in.
fact: The saiga is an antelope of the dry steppes of Kazakhstan, Mongolia and Russia whose big droopy nose filters dust in summer and warms freezing air in winter; a few hundred years ago it roamed as far west as Ukraine.
fact: In 2015 about 200,000 saigas died in a few weeks from bacteria that turned deadly in hot, damp weather, but with protection from hunting their numbers in Kazakhstan later grew to over a million.
fact: The pangolin is a mammal whose scales are made of keratin, like your fingernails, and when scared it rolls into a ball that even a lion finds hard to open.
fact: There are eight kinds of pangolin, four in Asia and four in Africa.
fact: A pangolin has no teeth; it licks up ants and termites with a very long sticky tongue anchored deep in its body near the hips, and small stones in its stomach help grind them.
fact: Pangolins are among the most smuggled mammals, killed for scales that are only keratin and cure nothing.
fact: The hagfish is an eel-shaped fish with no jaws; when grabbed it lets out slime that becomes a bucketful of goo in seconds and clogs an attacker's gills, and it ties itself in a knot to wipe the slime off.
fact: A hagfish has one main heart and several helper hearts, often counted as four in all; it can live for months without eating and helps clean the sea floor by eating dead whales and fish.
exercise: Where does the shoebill live? | In swamps of eastern and central Africa | On the frozen Arctic sea ice of Canada | In the dry red deserts of Australia | In mountain forests of central Europe and the Alps
exercise: How does a shoebill catch fish? | It waits almost without moving, then lunges | It dives straight down from high up in the sky | It chases the fish underwater for hours on end | It sucks them up with its big beak like a straw
exercise: How do shoebills greet each other? | By clattering their huge beaks | By singing long, sweet songs at dawn | By flapping their wings in water | By rubbing their long necks together
exercise: Which birds do scientists now think are the shoebill's closest relatives? | Pelicans and hammerkops | Eagles, hawks and big owls | Penguins and diving ducks | Parrots, pigeons and doves
exercise: Why are only a few thousand shoebills left? | Swamps are drained and eggs are taken | They eat each other | They cannot fly | They only lay eggs every hundred years
exercise: What is the star on the star-nosed mole's nose for? | Feeling for worms and insects in the dark | Smelling flowers on the grass above ground | Digging through hard soil like a spade | Scaring away foxes and owls that hunt it
exercise: How many fleshy rays are there on a star-nosed mole's star? | 22 | 5 | 8 | 100
exercise: How does a star-nosed mole smell underwater? | It blows bubbles onto things and sniffs them back in | It holds its breath and licks everything it bumps into | It smells through tiny holes in its front feet | It cannot smell underwater at all, it only touches things
exercise: What does the saiga's big nose do? | Filters dust in summer and warms freezing air in winter | Sucks up water like an elephant's trunk when it drinks | Digs for roots and grass under the deep snow | Makes loud trumpet calls to warn the whole herd of wolves
exercise: Where do saigas live today? | On the dry steppes of Kazakhstan, Mongolia and Russia | In the wet rainforests of Brazil and Peru | In the cold mountains of Scotland and northern Wales | On small islands far out in the middle of the Pacific Ocean
exercise: What killed about 200,000 saigas in 2015? | Bacteria that turned deadly in hot, damp weather | A huge snowstorm that buried the herds in one night | Hunters chasing them from helicopters | A volcano erupting near their winter grounds
exercise: What are pangolin scales made of? | Keratin, like your fingernails | Bone, like the bones of your skull | Stone swallowed with their food | Hard shell, like a garden snail's
exercise: What does a pangolin do when it is scared? | Rolls into a tight ball | Sprays a bad smell like a skunk | Plays dead with its tongue out | Climbs the nearest tree at once
exercise: How many kinds of pangolin are there? | Eight, four in Asia and four in Africa | One, and it lives in Africa only | Twenty, and all of them live in South America | Two, both living in Australia
exercise: How does a pangolin eat ants and termites without teeth? | A long sticky tongue, and stones in its stomach grind them | It swallows the whole nest of ants in one gulp | It crushes them first with its sharp scales | It sucks them up through its long nose like a straw, then chews
exercise: What does a hagfish do when a shark grabs it? | Lets out slime that clogs the shark's gills | Bites back hard with its huge sharp teeth | Turns bright red to frighten the shark | Swims away much faster than any shark can follow
exercise: How does a hagfish clean off its own slime? | It ties its body in a knot and slides the knot along | It rubs itself clean on a rough rock | It waits for the sea current to wash it off | It lets small cleaner fish eat the slime off its skin
exercise: How many hearts is a hagfish often said to have? | Four, one main heart and helpers | One, in the middle, just like a human | Ten, one in each part of its body | None, its blood is pushed by its muscles
test: «shoebill's a scary killer bird, it'd attack you on sight» What is right? | No, it is a shy bird that keeps away from people | Yes, it hunts people who walk into its swamps at night | Yes, it is the most dangerous bird in the world | No, because it is too weak to move at all
test: Why does a hunter that waits, like the shoebill, need patience more than speed? | It must stay still until a fish comes close, then lunge once | It is too slow to catch anything that swims away from it | Fish swim straight up to it and want to be eaten | Waiting a long time in the sun makes its huge beak sharper
test: Count them: a girl draws a star-nosed mole correctly. How many fleshy rays should she draw on each side of the star? | 11 | 22 | 5 | 44
test: How could scientists test whether the star-nosed mole is really smelling underwater and not just touching? | Put a smell behind a screen it cannot touch and see if it still finds the food | Ask the mole to point at the food with its nose | Paint its nose a new colour and see if it still likes worms | Take all the water out of the pond and watch it dig
test: In a cartoon, a mole pops up wearing big glasses and a hard hat. What does a real star-nosed mole use to find food in the dark? | A star of touch sensors and sniffs of bubbles | Big glasses that help its tiny eyes see | A little torch strapped to its head | Loud shouting that scares the worms out of the soil
test: «saigas died out in 2015, 200,000 gone» What is the truth? | About 200,000 died, but with protection they grew to over a million | Yes, they are extinct and none are left anywhere | No, none died at all, the whole story was made up by news reporters | They all left Kazakhstan and moved to Ukraine
test: Why is living in a giant herd both safe and risky for saigas? | A herd protects from hunters, but one disease can hit huge numbers at once | Herds are always safe, because wolves never attack big groups | Herds are always dangerous, because the saigas fight | Herds make the saigas slower and easier to catch
test: «pangolin scales cure illness, my uncle read it» What would you answer? | The scales are only keratin, like fingernails, and cure nothing | Yes, they cure every illness if you grind them to powder | Yes, but only if they are eaten raw | No, they are made of poison and make you ill
test: Count them: a pangolin mother and two babies roll into balls. How many balls, and how many tongues do they have together? | 3 balls and 3 tongues | 1 ball and 3 tongues | 3 balls and 6 tongues | 6 balls and 3 tongues
test: Why does hagfish slime work better than spines against a shark? | It clogs the gills so the shark cannot breathe and spits it out | Spines would be far too heavy for a small hagfish to carry | Sharks love the taste of spines and eat them happily | The slime makes the hagfish invisible in the dark deep water
test: What would happen to the sea floor if there were no scavengers like hagfish? | Dead whales and fish would take much longer to be cleaned up | The sea water would slowly become sweeter and less salty | Many more sharks would be born every single year | The water near the bottom would freeze into ice
test: Why might a scavenger of the deep sea need to survive months without food? | Dead whales and fish fall rarely, so meals may be far apart | It does not like eating and only nibbles for fun | Food down there is poisonous, so it can only eat very little at a time | It only eats on its birthday, once every year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scales of the pangolin? | They are composed of keratin and possess no medicinal value | They are composed of bone and cure numerous diseases of the blood | They are composed of stone which is swallowed with food | They are composed of shell resembling that of a snail
exam: Identify the principal function of the star-shaped organ upon the snout of the star-nosed mole. | The detection of prey by touch in darkness | The excavation of soil in the manner of a spade | The production of sound to repel predators | The perception of colour beneath the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the defence of the hagfish. | It releases slime which obstructs the gills of an attacker | It inflicts wounds upon attackers with its poisonous spines | It assumes the colour of its surroundings | It emits a loud sound which deafens approaching predators
exam: Ascertain the course of events affecting the saiga antelope subsequent to the mass mortality of the year 2015. | Under protection from hunting its numbers in Kazakhstan exceeded one million | The species was rendered extinct and no individuals survived in any country | The surviving animals migrated across the sea to Australia | The population remained at fewer than one hundred animals
